1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Hi there. Today, we are super excited that browser-powered scanning is now on by default

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,600
in VIRP Suite. So, what does this mean? To answer that question, we need to unwind a

3
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:29,980
few steps. Crawling a website to discover its content and functionality is a hard problem.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,220
It used to be easy. Burp Spider was first released in 2004. And this took a very simple approach.

5
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:48,400
It fetched each URL in turn, parsed the response to find new links, and added them to the queue.

6
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:54,120
This simple approach worked pretty well at the time, and it remained at the core of Burp

7
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:59,980
Spider-ing feature for many years. Crawling in this way is a good idea of

8
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:07,760
the way that the web is very fast. But it doesn't deal with numerous features of modern websites.

9
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:15,360
Things like application state, CSERV tokens, overloaded or volatile URLs.

10
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:22,980
Burp 2 was released in 2018. This came with a brand new crawling engine with a totally different

11
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:30,980
approach. Burp's crawler now navigates like a user, always working from the start URL and following links.

12
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:37,860
This is slower, but it solves the main issues that Burp Spider couldn't deal with.

13
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:45,640
Burp's crawler is always walking the right path to get to content means that the application is in the right state to proceed.

14
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:50,080
Burp deals with CSERV tokens that change each time a request is made.

15
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:58,320
Burp can handle URLs that are volatile and cases where the same URL does different things on different occasions.

16
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,320
Burp's crawler is a good idea of the way that the user can handle the user's crawler.

17
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:02,320
Burp is a good idea of the user's crawler. It's a good idea of the user's crawler.

18
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:03,320
Burp is a good idea of the user's crawler. It's a good idea of the user's crawler.

19
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:04,320
Burp is a good idea of the user's crawler. It's a good idea of the user's crawler.

20
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:05,320
It's a good idea of the user's crawler. It's a good idea of the user's crawler.

21
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:06,320
It's a good idea of the user's crawler. It's a good idea of the user's crawler.

22
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:07,320
It's a good idea of the user's crawler. It's a good idea of the user's crawler.

23
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:12,320
And this is the limitation that is addressed by browser-powered scanning.

24
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:19,320
Burp now uses an embedded Chromium browser for all navigation during both crawl and audit.

25
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:27,320
This deals with JavaScript. And in principle, it can handle pretty much anything that a browser can deal with.

26
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:35,320
For example, some applications have a navigational UI that is built dynamically with JavaScript

27
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:38,320
and not present in the raw HTML.

28
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:47,320
Burp now loads the page, executes the script, builds the UI, and can navigate on.

29
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:54,320
Some applications modify requests dynamically through on-click handlers or other JavaScript events.

30
00:02:54,320 --> 00:03:01,320
Because Burp now navigates using a browser, these requests get modified in the normal way as needed.

31
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:07,320
So Burp now deals nicely with common situations like these.

32
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:15,320
Following on from the Burp 2 improvements, browser-powered scanning is a huge step forward for Burp Scanner.

33
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:24,320
In many applications, browser-powered scanning will reach a lot more attack surface and find more vulnerabilities.

34
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:30,320
If you're a bug bounty hunter, everyone will already have scanned your targets using Burp Scanner.

35
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:36,320
But they'll have missed bugs that can't be found without browser-powered scanning.

36
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:39,320
Now might be a good time to look again.

37
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:43,320
So are there any downsides to browser-powered scanning?

38
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:54,320
Well, using a browser does use more system resources, so you might see Burp scans placing more load on your machine than before.

39
00:03:54,320 --> 00:04:00,320
We've taken various steps to mitigate this and to minimize the resource impact.

40
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:04,320
Certainly, you shouldn't see Burp overwhelming your machine.

41
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:13,320
But overall, with today's applications, the only way to effectively scan them is using a browser.

42
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:17,320
So the extra load is worth it.

43
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:21,320
You might wonder about security issues with using an embedded browser.

44
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:23,320
So did we.

45
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:25,320
The browser sandbox is enabled by default.

46
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:33,320
We update Chromium frequently, and there's an option to disable the browser altogether if you prefer not to use it.

47
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:37,320
So what's our roadmap for browser-powered scanning?

48
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:44,320
Currently, Burp deals well with links and forms-based navigation in JavaScript-heavy applications.

49
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:47,320
We're planning some more enhancements.

50
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:53,320
Firstly, in future, Burp will handle other navigational elements, not just links and forms,

51
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:57,320
but anything with an on-click handler that causes navigation.

52
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:03,320
Secondly, in future, Burp will handle navigational events that update the DOM

53
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:06,320
but don't cause a round trip to the server.

54
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:10,320
This will give us full support for single-page applications.

55
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,320
And coming soon?

56
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:13,320
Recorded logins.

57
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:18,320
You'll be able to guide Burp through complex login sequences to deal with situations

58
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:24,320
where just configuring a username or password isn't sufficient.

59
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:31,320
Aside from all of that, we have big plans for browser-powered security testing in general.

60
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:37,320
In the past at Portswigger, we've often pioneered new techniques to enhance Burp Scanner

61
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:40,320
and then later exposed those for manual testers.

62
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:43,320
One example of that is Burp Collaborator.

63
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:47,320
And we'll do this again with browser-powered testing.

64
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:52,320
We plan to build manual tooling on top of the same technology and engine.

65
00:05:52,320 --> 00:06:01,320
This will expose the same power to manual testers in tools like Burp Repeater and Intruder.

66
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:07,320
You'll be able to record any sequence using a browser by a Burp proxy.

67
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:13,320
Burp will capture the actual user actions alongside the resulting HTTP traffic.

68
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:20,320
When you send a request to Burp's manual tools, Burp will have access to the associated sequence of actions.

69
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:28,320
It'll be able to replay that sequence using a browser and then modify the relevant request that you're interested in.

70
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:35,320
So you'll be able to just send a request to Repeater or Intruder and work on that request in the normal way,

71
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:47,320
while in the background, Burp automatically deals with logins, maintaining session, CSERF tokens, stateful sequences, and other challenges.

72
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:54,320
All of this without any configuration of macros or session handling rules or anything else.

73
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:58,320
We would love to hear your feedback.

74
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:02,320
We'd like to hear how browser-powered scanning works for you,

75
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:07,320
and also any other ideas you have for using these techniques in manual testing tools.

76
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:08,320
Thank you very much.

77
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:09,320
Thank you very much.

